<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON MISTAKES
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Mistakes When Converting GMT to ACST</h2>
  
  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 1: Forgetting the 30-Minute Fractional Offset</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Adding 9 hours instead of 9 hours and 30 minutes is the single most common error, causing 30-minute delays for international calls.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 2: Confusing British Summer Time (BST) with GMT</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      GMT is UTC+0. During UK summer (March to October), London switches to BST (UTC+1). When comparing UK summer time to ACST, the gap narrows to 8.5 hours.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 3: Date Rollover Miscalculations for Afternoon GMT</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Because ACST is 9.5 hours ahead of GMT, any GMT timestamp after 2:30 PM GMT occurs on the next calendar day in Central Australia.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

